For a standard GraphQL server with introspection enabled, you can retrieve all interfaces via GraphQL’s introspection system.

### 1. Basic introspection query for interfaces

Send this query (e.g., via curl, Postman, GraphiQL, or your client) to the GraphQL HTTP endpoint:

```graphql
query {
  __schema {
    types {
      kind
      name
      description
      interfaces {
        name
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Then filter client-side for `kind === "INTERFACE"`.

### 2. More targeted query: only interfaces

If your GraphQL server supports more complex filtering, you can just pull out interfaces like this and then filter in code:

```graphql
query InterfacesOnly {
  __schema {
    types {
      kind
      name
      description
      fields {
        name
        type {
          kind
          name
          ofType {
            kind
            name
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Then in your client (JS example):

```js
const interfaces = data.__schema.types.filter(t => t.kind === "INTERFACE");
```

### 3. Get details of a specific interface

If you know the interface name:

```graphql
query {
  __type(name: "YourInterfaceName") {
    kind
    name
    description
    fields {
      name
      type {
        kind
        name
        ofType {
          kind
          name
        }
      }
    }
    possibleTypes {
      name
      kind
    }
  }
}
```

### 4. Notes / caveats

- Introspection must be enabled on the server; some production servers disable it.
- Some tools (GraphiQL, Apollo Studio, GraphQL Playground) internally run an introspection query and show you interfaces in their schema explorer UI. If you’re just exploring, using one of those may be easier than writing raw queries.

If you tell me which language/client you’re using (e.g., Node + Apollo, Python + gql), I can show a concrete code snippet for fetching and listing interfaces.

